<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carlo Cattaneo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1801">1801</a>–<a href="/years/1869">1869</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carlo Cattaneo was an Italian philosopher, economist and federalist. During the Five Days of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in March 1848 he led the council of war that directed the uprising against the Austrian garrison. He opposed handing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, favouring a federal republican <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and after the Austrian return he lived in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carlo-cattaneo.jpg" alt="Carlo Cattaneo" width="312" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edoardo Matania (1847-1929) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Matania_Edoardo_-_Ritratto_giovanile_di_Carlo_Cattaneo_-_xilografia_-_1887.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cattaneo was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> on 15 June 1801, the son of Melchiorre Cattaneo, a goldsmith, and Maria Antonia Sangiorgi. He studied law at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-pavia">University of Pavia</a> and graduated in 1824. A republican by conviction, he took part in his youth in the Carbonari movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>. He met the philosopher Giandomenico Romagnosi, whose stress on practical solutions and interdisciplinary work drew him, and hoped to turn the Italian people away from romanticism and rhetoric towards the positive sciences.</p><p>In 1839 he founded the review Il Politecnico in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> to set out these ideas. In 1835 he married his longtime fiancée Anne Pyne Woodcock, and from 1840 to 1848 he lived at the Palazzo Gavazzi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>During the Five Days of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> (18-22 March 1848), Cattaneo formed a council of war with the young democrats Enrico Cernuschi, Giulio Terzaghi and Giorgio Clerici, with its headquarters at Palazzo Taverna in via Bigli. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-radetzky-von-radetz">Field Marshal Radetzky</a> sounded out the rebels about terms, some leaders were ready for an armistice that would let Piedmontese troops arrive, but Cattaneo demanded the complete evacuation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>. On 21 March he again refused an armistice, saying it was better to die of hunger than on the gallows.</p><p>When the Austrians returned in August 1848 he fled to the canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ticino">Ticino</a>. From 1852 to 1865 he taught philosophy at the new lyceum of Lugano and shaped its teaching. He wrote a history of the 1848 revolution and the Archivio triennale delle cose d'Italia (1850-1855), revived Il Politecnico in 1860, and in 1858 received an honorary Swiss bourgeoisie from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-qing">Grand Council</a> of Ticino.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Cattaneo opposed Camillo Benso di <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camillo-benso-count-of-cavour">Cavour</a> for his unitarian views and for ceding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a> and Savoy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>. In 1860 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a> called him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> to help govern the Neapolitan provinces, but he would not accept union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> without local autonomy. Elected office required an oath to the monarchy, which he would not take; in 1868 he agreed to stand for the Chamber of Deputies but withdrew at the last moment. He died on 6 February 1869 at Castagnola in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>.</p><p>Some have judged him too bitter a partisan, and his ideas of local autonomy were thought inopportune at a time when unity seemed essential. Gaetano Salvemini counted him among the men of genius of 19th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, with Giacomo Leopardi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camillo-benso-count-of-cavour">Cavour</a> and Francesco de Sanctis.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Carlo Cattaneo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Giuseppe Mazzini and <b>Carlo Cattaneo</b> wanted a federal republic, which proved too extreme for most nationalists.</blockquote><a href="/movement/unification-of-italy">Unification of Italy · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>A provisional government headed by the mayor Gabrio Casati took power, while the democrat <b>Carlo Cattaneo</b> led the council of war during the fighting.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-milan">Provisional Government of Milan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Castagnola-Cassarate, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.47,9.19&amp;year=1845" aria-label="Milan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4768 560 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Carlo Cattaneo"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5218 830h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5218 830h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.47,9.19&amp;year=1845">What was here in 1845? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
